{"event":"trap-result","runId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0/branch-5","trapId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0/branch-5/trap-0","ordinal":0,"result":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.17930","title":"Token2Tile: Token-Wise Sparse Attention with Tile Execution through a Low-Rank Space for Video DiTs","authors":"Xuecheng Wu , Aichen Feng , Jinyan Chen , Qingjie Zhao , Julien Lai","submitted":"13 Nov 2026","abstract":"Attention is essential for modeling spatio-temporal dependencies in video diffusion transformers, but its quadratic cost makes it a major inference bottleneck. Its high sparsity offers an opportunity to accelerate generation by focusing computation on important interactions. However, accuracy and execution favor different granularities: each query attends to its own key tokens, whereas GPU kernels work on tiles. We present Token2Tile, a training-free sparse attention operator that combines per-query scoring and a per-query residual with tile execution. An online low-rank space, computed directly from queries and keys to preserve attention logits, makes clustering, scoring, and residual approximation efficient. Token2Tile unites query-specific selections to allocate exact work across query clusters, then packs the selected keys into GPU tiles. On MiniMax-H3 and Wan2.1-14B, Token2Tile delivers 1.48-1.54x end-to-end speedup over dense FlashAttention-3 with VBench scores within 0.5 points of dense. At comparable speeds on H3, it improves PSNR over SOL and SubBlock by 5.5-6.8 dB; on Wan, it exceeds SOL by 4.6-4.8 dB while running faster.","numbers":["On MiniMax-H3 and Wan2.1-14B, Token2Tile delivers 1.48-1.54x end-to-end speedup over dense FlashAttention-3 with VBench scores within 0.5 points of dense.","At comparable speeds on H3, it improves PSNR over SOL and SubBlock by 5.5-6.8 dB; on Wan, it exceeds SOL by 4.6-4.8 dB while running faster."],"method":["We present Token2Tile, a training-free sparse attention operator that combines per-query scoring and a per-query residual with tile execution."],"caveats":["However, accuracy and execution favor different granularities: each query attends to its own key tokens, whereas GPU kernels work on tiles."],"topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","diffusion"],"score":10,"fullText":true,"readChars":5930,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17930","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.17930"}}
{"event":"branch-done","runId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0/branch-5","parentForkId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0","branch":5,"outcome":{"ok":true,"value":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.17930","title":"Token2Tile: Token-Wise Sparse Attention with Tile Execution through a Low-Rank Space for Video DiTs","authors":"Xuecheng Wu , Aichen Feng , Jinyan Chen , Qingjie Zhao , Julien Lai","submitted":"13 Nov 2026","abstract":"Attention is essential for modeling spatio-temporal dependencies in video diffusion transformers, but its quadratic cost makes it a major inference bottleneck. Its high sparsity offers an opportunity to accelerate generation by focusing computation on important interactions. However, accuracy and execution favor different granularities: each query attends to its own key tokens, whereas GPU kernels work on tiles. We present Token2Tile, a training-free sparse attention operator that combines per-query scoring and a per-query residual with tile execution. An online low-rank space, computed directly from queries and keys to preserve attention logits, makes clustering, scoring, and residual approximation efficient. Token2Tile unites query-specific selections to allocate exact work across query clusters, then packs the selected keys into GPU tiles. On MiniMax-H3 and Wan2.1-14B, Token2Tile delivers 1.48-1.54x end-to-end speedup over dense FlashAttention-3 with VBench scores within 0.5 points of dense. At comparable speeds on H3, it improves PSNR over SOL and SubBlock by 5.5-6.8 dB; on Wan, it exceeds SOL by 4.6-4.8 dB while running faster.","numbers":["On MiniMax-H3 and Wan2.1-14B, Token2Tile delivers 1.48-1.54x end-to-end speedup over dense FlashAttention-3 with VBench scores within 0.5 points of dense.","At comparable speeds on H3, it improves PSNR over SOL and SubBlock by 5.5-6.8 dB; on Wan, it exceeds SOL by 4.6-4.8 dB while running faster."],"method":["We present Token2Tile, a training-free sparse attention operator that combines per-query scoring and a per-query residual with tile execution."],"caveats":["However, accuracy and execution favor different granularities: each query attends to its own key tokens, whereas GPU kernels work on tiles."],"topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","diffusion"],"score":10,"fullText":true,"readChars":5930,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17930","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.17930"}}}
